Under what conditions can the following code be parallelized? If these conditions are met,
          is there a way to write the code for better parallelization? (2)
            for (i=0; i<100; i++)
                      for (j=2; j<100; j++)
                            a[i][j] = f(a[i][j]) + a[i][j-2];

Which loop can be parallelized and under which conditions? Does the overhead of the
       runtime impact the parallelization of the loops? Which is the best way to parallelize this
      code on multi-core systems? (2)
            for (i=0; i<1000; i++)
                      for (j=0; j<4; j++)
                            a[i][j] = f(a[i][j]) + a[i][j];

Where and under what conditions can the following code be parallelized? Is there a way to
       write the code for better parallelization if these conditions are met? (2)
            for (i=0; i<100; i++) {
                      for (j=1; j<100; j++)
                         a[i][0] += f(a[i-5][j]);
                      for (j=0; j<100; j++)
                            b[i][j] += f(b[i][j]);
          }

Where and under what conditions can the following code be parallelized? Is there a way to
       write the code for better parallelization if these conditions are met? Assume that a and b
       store integers. (2)
            for (i=3; i<100; i++) {
                      for (j=1; j<100; j++)
                         a[i][0] *= f(a[i][j]);
                      for (j=0; j<100; j++)
                          b[i-3][j] += f(b[i][j]);
          }

Consider the following nested loop:
        for (j = 0; j < 100; j++) {
            for (i = 1; i < 100; i++) {
                a[i][j] = f(a[i-1][j]);
            }
        }
       Evaluate the cache-friendliness of this code. How does the memory layout of the array
        affect performance, and what changes could improve cache utilization? Is it still
        parallelizable? How? Justify your answer. (2)

Consider the following nested loop:
        for (j = 0; j < 100; j++) {
            b[j] = f1(a[j]);
        }
        for (j = 99; j >= 0; j--) {
            a[j] = f2(b[j]);
        }
      Which loop can be parallelized and under which conditions (j is declared as an integer)? Is
       there a way to improve the code? Is the OpenMP nowait clause a possible option? Justify
       your answers. (2)

Consider the following nested loop:
        for (i=0; i<100; i++) {
          for (j=0; j<100; j++)
            b[i][j] += f(a[i][j]);
          for (j=99; j>=0; j++)
            a[i][j] += f(b[i][j]);
        }
      Which loop can be parallelized and under which conditions (i and j are declared as
       integers)? Is there a way to improve the code? Is the OpenMP nowait clause a possible
       option? Justify your answers. (2)
